{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1629 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "eeaa705ffb8dcb84334a563e77fdb9c9b3d74a73470c61b16f923dabeec2582b" :edb/db-before-t 1628 :edb/db-after-t 1629 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534941 50 #inst "2026-10-08T11:37:11.326Z" 13194139534941 true] [17592186321097 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321098 13194139534941 false] [17592186321097 1017 #edb/ref 17592186321372 13194139534941 true] [17592186321097 1100 "Given that $g(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nFind the set of real values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist. Choose the displayed set that expresses the required condition on $x^2+y^2$ explicitly as a strict positivity inequality." 13194139534941 true] [17592186321097 1100 "Given that $g(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nfind the set of values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist." 13194139534941 false] [17592186321097 1145 "First, notice that $g(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of $g$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{g}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\n$$\n\nAs for the partial derivatives, we have the following.\n\n- The function ${g}_{x}(x,y)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{x}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{x}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\n$$\n\n- The function ${g}_{y}(x,y)=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ is defined only when ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0.$ So, the domain of ${g}_{y}$ is \n\n$$\n{D}_{{g}_{y}}={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the partials of $g$ exist on the set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{g}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{x}}\\cap {D}_{{g}_{y}} & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}\\cap {(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0} \\\\  & ={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534941 false] [17592186321097 1145 "For real $x$ and $y$, each square is nonnegative, so $x^2+y^2\\ge 0$. Their sum equals zero exactly when $x=y=0$. Consequently,\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2\\ne 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x^2+y^2>0.\n$$\n\nThe logarithm in $g(x,y)=x\\ln(x^2+y^2)$ requires $x^2+y^2>0$. This same condition makes the denominators in both supplied partial derivatives nonzero. On this open set the logarithm and rational terms are differentiable, so both partial derivatives exist. At the origin the original function is undefined, so its partial derivatives do not exist there.\n\nThus the domain where all partial derivatives exist is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x^2+y^2>0\\}=\\mathbb R^2\\setminus\\{(0,0)\\}.\n$$\n\nThe choices using $x^2+y^2\\ne 0$ (c) and $x^2+y^2>0$ (e) describe exactly the same domain. The question explicitly requests the strict positivity form, so choose e. Choice c is an equivalent description of the domain, but it does not express the condition as the requested positivity inequality." 13194139534941 true] [17592186321372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321099 13194139534941 true] [17592186321372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321100 13194139534941 true] [17592186321372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321101 13194139534941 true] [17592186321372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321102 13194139534941 true] [17592186321372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186321103 13194139534941 true] [17592186321372 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534941 true] [17592186321372 1041 "selection" 13194139534941 true] [17592186321372 1104 #edb/ref 17592186321102 13194139534941 true] [17592186321372 1110 #uuid "1a0278f4-7ef2-5c9f-a5d9-85a1f6bca7e8" 13194139534941 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-72005/mathematical-correction-v1/selection" 17592186321372}}
